## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to design data-rights enforcement in OTCA with cross-region flows and immutable stores.

## Key Concepts
- Deletion token propagation
- Tombstone and GC semantics for Kafka
- Immutable storage considerations (Parquet/S3)
- Auditability and legal holds
- Schema-versioning to avoid replay risks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: emit tombstone and propagate deletion token
function deleteTenantData(tenantId){
  emitKafkaTombstone(tenantId);
  purgeDerivedFeatures(tenantId);
  markParquetShardsDeleted(tenantId);
  updateCentralRegistry(tenantId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test deletion under concurrent deletion requests?
- How would you handle multi-tenant data that migrates regions after deletion?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical OTCA telemetry path focusing on data-access events in a cross-tenant editor, including privacy constraints and per-tenant residency. It also exercises basic RBAC considerations and test planning.

## Key Concepts

- OTCA telemetry path from client to data lake
- Per-tenant residency and 30-day retention
- Data minimization and redaction at ingestion
- Kafka streaming and Parquet partitions by tenant/date
- RBAC-based query filtering for cross-tenant isolation
- Practical test plans for privacy, auditing, and retention

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo ingestion redaction
function ingest(rec){
  rec.user_id_hash = hash(rec.user_id);
  rec.tenant_id = rec.tenant_id;
  delete rec.email;
  return rec;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate per-tenant RBAC at scale?
- How would you simulate privacy and retention audits in CI/CD?

